\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a finite set $P\subset\mathbb R^2$ of distinct points, let
\[
 u(P)=\#\{\{x,y\}\subseteq P:\ x\ne y,\ \|x-y\|_2=1\}.
\]
Pairs are unordered and the distance is Euclidean. Define $u(n)=\max_{|P|=n}u(P)$, with the maximum over all planar $n$-point sets.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine the maximum $u(n)$ for general $n\in\mathbb N$, and its sharp asymptotic order as $n\to\infty$. No lattice, general-position, or algebraicity restriction is imposed on $P$. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20579v1}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Among n distinct points in the Euclidean plane, what is the greatest possible number of pairs exactly one unit apart, and how does this maximum grow?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1383 (GEOM-024). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Will Sawin, \emph{An explicit lower bound for the unit distance problem} (2026), arXiv:2605.20579v1, Theorem 1 and introductory discussion, p. 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20579v1}.
